  "analysis": [
    {
      "label": "Context",
      "text": "Argentina led 2–1 midway through the second half. France had the ball but no way through the middle, so the attack went down the left and into the box from wide."
    },
    {
      "label": "Build-up",
      "text": "Kanté carried it out of midfield and Matuidi took it on down the left. Hernández was left free to cross, which pulled Argentina's defenders towards their own goal line."
    },
    {
      "label": "Key moment",
      "text": "The clearing header went up and backwards rather than away, and dropped towards the edge of the box on the opposite side to the cross — the space Argentina's defence had just vacated."
    },
    {
      "label": "The technique",
      "text": "The ball arrived from Pavard's left, so instead of controlling it, Pavard opened the hips and struck across the ball with the outside of the boot, sending it curling back towards the far corner."
    }
  ],
  "lessons": [
    {
      "label": "Attack the second ball",
      "text": "Clearances from crosses usually drop on the edge of the box. Someone standing there, ready to hit it first time, turns a defended cross into a chance."
    },
    {
      "label": "Open the hips",
      "text": "A ball arriving across the body can be struck side on, without a control touch. Turning the hips towards the target is what keeps the shot down and on target."
    },
    {
      "label": "Hit across the ball",
      "text": "Contact through the outside half sends a side-on volley curling away from the keeper, which is why it finishes in the far corner rather than at the near post."
    },
    {
      "label": "Full-backs can shoot",
      "text": "Pavard is a defender, arriving in a position nobody marks. Defenders who follow the play into the box get chances that nobody is expecting."
    }
  ],
